geoloc.bin — local IP-prefix → coordinate table for Seer's geo-DNS
==================================================================

Consumed by  crates/hive-cloud/src/geoip.rs  (include_bytes!, zero-copy).
Produced by  scripts/gen-geo-table.py       (not part of the build).

Regenerate:

    curl -sO https://download.db-ip.com/free/dbip-city-lite-YYYY-MM.csv.gz
    scripts/gen-geo-table.py dbip-city-lite-YYYY-MM.csv.gz \
        crates/hive-cloud/assets/geoloc.bin

Current contents
----------------
source snapshot : dbip-city-lite-2026-07 (8 053 351 ranges, city precision)
fold tolerance  : 800 km
rows            : 804 441 IPv4 (/0-aligned run table) + 81 040 IPv6 (/32 keys)
size            : 5 312 918 bytes (32-byte header + 6 bytes/row)

Attribution (required by the source licence)
--------------------------------------------
IP geolocation data by DB-IP (https://db-ip.com), "IP to City Lite" database,
licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC-BY-4.0):
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

CC-BY explicitly permits the redistribution this file performs. MaxMind's
GeoLite2 is technically equivalent but its licence does not, which is why it is
not the input.

Why this is committed rather than downloaded
--------------------------------------------
The whole point is that geolocating a DNS client requires no third-party call at
run time. A build step that fetched the CSV would move that dependency from the
data path to the build path instead of deleting it, and a node that fetched it
at boot would still be a node that fails to route well when a free service is
unreachable. Committed, the table works on a node with no egress at all.
